00:00:07.749 --> 00:00:09.810 align:start position:0%
we're very good at throwing even our  closest living relatives chimpanzees  can't match the speed and accuracy of  our pitches so how and when did this  uniquely human skill evolve scientists  at Harvard University invited college  league baseball players into their lab  each player made eight to ten throws at  a target while their movements were  recorded by motion capture cameras like  the ones used in Hollywood the data  revealed it's all in the shoulder the  shoulder acts like a slingshot as the  arm rotates backwards the surrounding  ligaments tendons and muscles stretch  and store elastic energy it's this  energy that powers the forward throw  catapulting the ball towards its target  the rotation is remarkably fast in fact  it's the fastest motion the human body  produces a chimps ability to throw is  limited by its high shrugged shoulders  our shoulders sit lower and face  outwards giving us a greater range of  motion and allowing us to store far more  elastic energy in the shoulder notice  how this pitcher is able to rotate his  arm more effectively than the chimp  scientists think that our ability to  throw evolved about two million years  ago in our ancestor Homo erectus back  then it was used for hunting now when we  do all our hunting in supermarkets we  use the same skill for sport or having  fun in the park
 

